1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,272
Americans love their lawns. Every weekend we mow, blow, and trim to perfection.

2
00:00:13,273 --> 00:00:15,960
Turning the yard

3
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:18,890
into a 100 billion dollar a year industry.

4
00:00:18,891 --> 00:00:21,960
We've got tillers, lawn tractors, chainsaws,

5
00:00:21,961 --> 00:00:26,180
trimmers, blowers, every gadget you can possibly imagine.

6
00:00:26,181 --> 00:00:28,920
But the ultimate backyard oasis isn't

7
00:00:28,921 --> 00:00:31,080
just about grass anymore.

8
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:33,120
We're gonna have 10,000 gallons flowing through here.

9
00:00:33,121 --> 00:00:34,800
It's gonna be very, very beautiful.

10
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:38,020
You'll never look at your yard the same way again

11
00:00:38,021 --> 00:00:39,300
on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:07,240
In yards across America, the lawn is a labor of love.

13
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:10,580
The basis of this national grassroots obsession?

14
00:01:10,581 --> 00:01:11,580
Turfgrass.

15
00:01:11,581 --> 00:01:17,980
Turfgrass is a dense cover of grass and roots in the top layer of the soil.

16
00:01:17,981 --> 00:01:23,320
Today it's estimated there are about 50 million acres of it planted in the United States.

17
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:28,900
And that 81% of Americans have a lawn.

18
00:01:28,901 --> 00:01:31,700
The grass is typically a permanent fixture,

19
00:01:31,701 --> 00:01:36,200
but one unusual lawn was built for travel.

20
00:01:36,201 --> 00:01:39,240
The University of Phoenix Stadium is a dome,

21
00:01:39,241 --> 00:01:42,140
home to the NFL's Arizona Cardinals.

22
00:01:42,141 --> 00:01:43,740
But there's a problem.

23
00:01:43,741 --> 00:01:46,780
Beyond hosting football, it's also booked as a site

24
00:01:46,781 --> 00:01:49,460
for events that would pulverize a grass surface.

25
00:01:49,461 --> 00:01:52,520
They can bring in tractor pulls, big boy toys.

26
00:01:52,521 --> 00:01:54,560
I mean, you name it, we've had every kind of show

27
00:01:54,561 --> 00:01:55,700
in the world in here.

28
00:01:55,701 --> 00:01:58,200
But you can't do it if you have a field in here.

29
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:02,840
The solution is a new twist on indoor-outdoor carpet.

30
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,800
But this carpet is made of real grass.

31
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:10,260
As the turf flourishes outside in the Arizona sun,

32
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,960
spot watering and computer-controlled irrigation keeps it green.

33
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:18,360
The field is in one piece, not segmented.

34
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:22,480
So to move it inside, the turf can't be rolled in sections.

35
00:02:22,481 --> 00:02:29,960
Instead, when game time looms, it rolls into the dome in one hulking mass of grass.

36
00:02:29,961 --> 00:02:38,260
The two-acre field sits in a massive tray in layers, much like a birthday cake.

37
00:02:38,261 --> 00:02:43,240
A steel support structure and metal decking support the tray.

38
00:02:43,241 --> 00:02:49,720
The plastic tray sits one inch above the support structure to allow for water drainage below.

39
00:02:49,721 --> 00:02:53,720
The turf grows atop a one-foot-thick root zone.

40
00:02:53,721 --> 00:02:57,720
This layer is a mixture of sand and reinforcing fibers

41
00:02:57,721 --> 00:03:01,720
that provides optimum stability for the athletes.

42
00:03:01,721 --> 00:03:05,720
Moving this 19-million-pound chunk of sod

43
00:03:05,721 --> 00:03:09,720
takes surprisingly little power. An extension cord

44
00:03:09,721 --> 00:03:13,720
and a 13-track rail system are all that's needed.

45
00:03:13,721 --> 00:03:17,720
A utility trench allows workers to monitor the move from below.

46
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:26,720
There's our warning signal for the field moving.

47
00:03:26,721 --> 00:03:31,720
Now we get to listen to that for 70 minutes.

48
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:40,720
Here we're looking at the gearbox to drive the wheel.

49
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:43,720
Here's a one horsepower motor.

50
00:03:43,721 --> 00:03:48,720
There are 76 one horsepower motors driving this field at this time.

51
00:03:48,721 --> 00:03:53,720
We connect it with two electrical cords.

52
00:03:53,721 --> 00:03:58,720
We come halfway in, we stop, unplug the cords,

53
00:03:58,721 --> 00:04:00,720
take them to the other end,

54
00:04:00,721 --> 00:04:03,720
we plug them in back down the other end,

55
00:04:03,721 --> 00:04:06,720
and then we'll come in the second half of the way.

56
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:26,800
For all you homeowners saddled with a lawn that can't move, the real action lies in what

57
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:28,220
you move on top of it.

58
00:04:28,221 --> 00:04:42,221
From zero to seven miles per hour in seconds flat, with engines as powerful as 30 horsepower,

59
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:47,320
The riding lawn mower has been the big dog of the yard

60
00:04:47,321 --> 00:04:48,760
for decades.

61
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:53,180
In the 1950s, the riding mower was the new status symbol.

62
00:04:53,181 --> 00:04:56,520
It was advertised as a little car.

63
00:04:56,521 --> 00:05:01,600
Some of them had martini glass holders, all kinds of things.

64
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:03,860
And everybody wanted a riding mower.

65
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:04,561
They were fun.

66
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,100
Say hello to the new big dog.

67
00:05:09,101 --> 00:05:13,100
It's called ZTR, or zero turn radius.

68
00:05:13,101 --> 00:05:17,000
and is taking a big bite out of the time it takes to mow the lawn.

69
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:18,340
They are more productive.

70
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:20,160
They get the job done faster.

71
00:05:20,161 --> 00:05:23,660
From a homeowner standpoint, they're more fun to operate.

72
00:05:23,661 --> 00:05:25,360
Part of the fun of a zero-turn mower

73
00:05:25,361 --> 00:05:28,240
is trading the steering wheel for a pair of levers.

74
00:05:28,241 --> 00:05:30,860
The levers control two independent motor drives

75
00:05:30,861 --> 00:05:32,100
that power the rear wheels.

76
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:40,040
This is a zero-turn lawnmower.

77
00:05:40,041 --> 00:05:43,000
The basic principle behind it, if you make a right-handed turn,

78
00:05:43,001 --> 00:05:45,920
Your right hand transmission is not going to work at all.

79
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:47,880
The left hand transmission does all the work.

80
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:52,620
If you notice, it turns in place.

81
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:56,600
The right hand wheel is not moving, but the left hand wheel is working completely.

82
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:05,880
You can go into much tighter places and then get back out without lots of extra maneuvering.

83
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:12,680
Or when you come up to a small tree, you can go around it one time and be done.

84
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,220
If a homeowner can go out and do the job in half the time, that's worth a lot.

85
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:23,880
In the centuries before the ZTR and its brethren,

86
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:28,040
turning a pasture into a lawn was a tedious and uneven proposition.

87
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:32,100
As 17th century English gardens gained popularity,

88
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:36,360
the owners began to employ small armies of workers wielding scythes.

89
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,980
It was a long stick with a large blade on the end of it.

90
00:06:39,981 --> 00:06:42,680
that an individual would swing back and forth,

91
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:45,840
and that's how the lawn or grass area would then be cut.

92
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:49,640
Very labor-intensive, took a good deal of skill

93
00:06:49,641 --> 00:06:51,740
to be able to use that over time,

94
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:54,380
especially if you're trying to get a low and level cut.

95
00:06:56,140 --> 00:06:59,560
In 1830, English inventor Edwin Budding

96
00:06:59,561 --> 00:07:02,240
envisioned a better way to get a low and level cut.

97
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:06,600
He built a large cast-iron machine

98
00:07:06,601 --> 00:07:09,020
with a revolving cutting cylinder at the front.

99
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:16,940
He called it a real lawnmower, and it's a design that lives on today.

100
00:07:16,941 --> 00:07:20,480
A real mower shears grass like a pair of scissors.

101
00:07:20,481 --> 00:07:24,300
A central shaft rotates a series of cutting blades.

102
00:07:24,301 --> 00:07:27,580
The turning blades come into contact with a bed knife.

103
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:32,080
The scissor action cuts the blades of grass.

104
00:07:32,081 --> 00:07:37,080
For decades, few people saw the need for a lawnmower, but in the United States, that

105
00:07:37,081 --> 00:07:38,080
changed.

106
00:07:38,081 --> 00:07:40,580
when the urban lawn first came into vogue.

107
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:47,440
Over 38 patents for real-type mowers were issued between 1868 and 1873.

108
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,440
At the turn of the century, several mower inventions added small steam

109
00:07:53,441 --> 00:07:55,600
and even gasoline-fueled engines.

110
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:59,980
Many inventors lay claim to the rotary lawnmower.

111
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:05,740
But in 1931, the Louisville Electric Manufacturing Company

112
00:08:05,741 --> 00:08:10,540
built and marketed one of the world's first.

113
00:08:10,541 --> 00:08:16,680
The rotary design ultimately became a standard by the 1960s.

114
00:08:16,681 --> 00:08:22,420
The rotary mower employs a single blade, revolving at around 2,800 revolutions per minute, to

115
00:08:22,421 --> 00:08:27,440
cut the grass and then discharge it through a chute.

116
00:08:27,441 --> 00:08:33,200
A mulching mower uses a higher deck without a chute to suspend the grass.

117
00:08:33,201 --> 00:08:36,200
A blade with a longer cutting edge repeatedly chops it to pieces

118
00:08:36,201 --> 00:08:38,620
and returns it as mulch back into the lawn.

119
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:42,900
It puts the nutrients from the grass blades,

120
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:46,460
the decomposition of the grass blades, back into the turf.

121
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:49,640
If you're bagging, you don't have to dispose of the clipping,

122
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:54,060
so there's the landfill issues and containment issues of grass clippings.

123
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:57,540
So how much do we love our lawns?

124
00:08:57,541 --> 00:09:04,540
Landscaping is more than a $100 billion a year industry.

125
00:09:04,541 --> 00:09:12,140
For decades, there's been one goal almost all homeowners across America have been striving

126
00:09:12,141 --> 00:09:15,140
towards.

127
00:09:15,141 --> 00:09:18,640
The perfect lawn.

128
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:23,140
Today's technology makes the back-breaking chores of working around the yard a little

129
00:09:23,141 --> 00:09:27,140
easier.

130
00:09:27,141 --> 00:09:32,500
Since 1907, the Toro Company has been one of the leading lawnmower makers in the United States.

131
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:39,300
The Toro Testing and Design Center in Bloomington, Minnesota, designs and builds mower prototypes.

132
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:45,120
The pieces are laser cut and assembled.

133
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:50,920
They're then subjected to a battery of tests that can last for years.

134
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:56,320
The engineers test for safety, performance, and durability.

135
00:09:57,140 --> 00:10:02,740
The standard durability drill is called the bump test.

136
00:10:02,741 --> 00:10:08,540
Here we try to simulate the entire life of the machine, which may be 3,000 hours,

137
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:13,440
but we try to accelerate the life by having the biggest possible bumps on what we call

138
00:10:13,441 --> 00:10:19,260
a roll dyno, which shocks the machine at a very regular basis, and we try to get the

139
00:10:19,261 --> 00:10:24,540
end result of life under the machine in a reasonable period of time, which might be

140
00:10:24,541 --> 00:10:26,540
a month or two months.

141
00:10:26,541 --> 00:10:37,160
Mowers are noisy, so Toro engineers are constantly trying to build a quieter machine.

142
00:10:37,161 --> 00:10:40,040
The sound test tries to pinpoint noise that can be eliminated.

143
00:10:40,041 --> 00:10:46,560
What we're looking for is objectionable sounds or noises that might be coming from the panels

144
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:53,200
that are on the machine, the engine, the fan, the hydraulics.

145
00:10:53,201 --> 00:10:57,840
While microphones measure external machine noise, a second test judges the effects on

146
00:10:57,841 --> 00:10:59,880
the operator's ears.

147
00:10:59,881 --> 00:11:04,520
This is the test where we try to measure what the sound level would be at the operator's

148
00:11:04,521 --> 00:11:11,460
ear and we try to stay within certain measurable limits.

149
00:11:11,461 --> 00:11:17,400
Not all yards are flat, Sotoro uses a tilt table test to determine machine rollover angles.

150
00:11:17,401 --> 00:11:22,960
It has two main purposes, one to check the parking brake of the machines and

151
00:11:22,961 --> 00:11:25,780
Second is to check the overall stability.

152
00:11:25,781 --> 00:11:29,220
We go up to first 16.7 degrees, which

153
00:11:29,221 --> 00:11:32,320
is the minimum level for parking breaks.

154
00:11:32,321 --> 00:11:34,780
And then we go all the way up to, in this particular case,

155
00:11:34,781 --> 00:11:38,720
25 degrees, which is the upper limit of stability.

156
00:11:38,721 --> 00:11:41,380
But even if your lawn is perfectly level,

157
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:43,420
what if there's something unexpected lurking

158
00:11:43,421 --> 00:11:46,580
beneath the grass?

159
00:11:46,581 --> 00:11:49,660
The drastic stake test is an alarming suburban vision

160
00:11:49,661 --> 00:11:51,360
of what could happen.

161
00:11:51,361 --> 00:11:55,140
is to represent the worst we could imagine a customer doing

162
00:11:55,141 --> 00:11:58,380
in their lawn if they hit the water main or a sprinkler

163
00:11:58,381 --> 00:12:02,040
head or other objects in the lawn.

164
00:12:02,041 --> 00:12:03,800
A one-inch diameter steel stake

165
00:12:03,801 --> 00:12:07,040
is positioned below ground level.

166
00:12:07,041 --> 00:12:09,480
The engineers engage the mower blade,

167
00:12:09,481 --> 00:12:12,120
move it over the impact zone, and then cringe.

168
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:17,580
And what we're after there is to make sure

169
00:12:17,581 --> 00:12:20,460
that there are no failures on the machine

170
00:12:20,461 --> 00:12:26,060
to make sure that the machine can still be used for the intended purpose, which is cutting grass.

171
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:33,140
These and dozens of other tests help engineers build safer and better mowers.

172
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:41,380
Toro has even tested an autonomous lawnmower with high-precision global positioning

173
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:44,040
and obstacle recognition capabilities.

174
00:12:44,041 --> 00:12:53,640
It represents one possible future option for commercial and municipal grounds and golf courses.

175
00:12:55,960 --> 00:12:59,080
But the future is now for the gadget-happy homeowner.

176
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:07,540
Luis Medina founded his company, Evatech, to build a mower for people who would rather do their yard work sitting down.

177
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:12,300
I used to cap my grass with a regular, typical push mower.

178
00:13:12,301 --> 00:13:18,260
And I wanted to come out with something easier to use, not just for me, but for my friends.

179
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:24,460
The Evatek machine puts mowing at your fingertips with wireless joystick control.

180
00:13:26,620 --> 00:13:30,760
Well, here we have the transmitter. It has two joysticks that controls a unit.

181
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:33,960
The transmitter sends the signal to the brain.

182
00:13:33,961 --> 00:13:40,900
the brain because the signal through a very sophisticated algorithm that makes the mower

183
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:43,900
go forward, reverse, and zero turning radius.

184
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,140
All that movement is powered by an electric hybrid engine.

185
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:54,460
A separate gasoline engine operates the cutting blade.

186
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:00,040
It even comes with its own seed spreader attachment.

187
00:14:00,041 --> 00:14:08,500
choosing a remote controlled lawnmower over a self-propelled or a ztr model is a lifestyle choice

188
00:14:08,501 --> 00:14:18,580
but there are other ways to beautify your lawn inside this greenhouse complex in

189
00:14:18,581 --> 00:14:23,260
Marysville Ohio even a snowstorm can't delay the future of the American lawn

190
00:14:23,261 --> 00:14:30,940
The grass breeders at Scott's Miracle-Gro company do more than just figure out how to

191
00:14:30,941 --> 00:14:32,328
keep your lawn green.

192
00:14:32,329 --> 00:14:37,020
They're conducting experiments aimed at re-engineering the lawn itself.

193
00:14:37,021 --> 00:14:43,540
As public water supplies dwindle and fuel prices rise, they're trying to breed grasses that need

194
00:14:43,541 --> 00:14:45,516
less water and less mowing.

195
00:14:45,517 --> 00:14:50,200
These greenhouse facilities allow us to conduct some of the more

196
00:14:50,201 --> 00:14:54,400
controlled and precise experiments that we do with the Scots company. We can

197
00:14:54,401 --> 00:14:58,360
control light, we can control the soils that we use, we can control the irrigation,

198
00:14:58,361 --> 00:15:02,620
the nutrition that we're providing, and really simulate just about any

199
00:15:02,621 --> 00:15:07,360
environment that you'd find any place in the world. The greenhouse contains

200
00:15:07,361 --> 00:15:12,520
multiple rooms, each designed to simulate different climates while growing a

201
00:15:12,521 --> 00:15:18,760
variety of grasses. In one room they're developing slow growing or slow mow grass

202
00:15:18,761 --> 00:15:22,760
to give lawn mowers a break.

203
00:15:22,761 --> 00:15:25,760
These grasses are bred to possess fewer of the hormones

204
00:15:25,761 --> 00:15:28,760
that encourage plant cells to elongate.

205
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:32,760
And then they will cross those and go through this plant breeding

206
00:15:32,761 --> 00:15:35,760
that can take up to 12 years to develop a grass

207
00:15:35,761 --> 00:15:37,760
that will then give a desirable lawn

208
00:15:37,761 --> 00:15:39,760
without requiring a lot of mowing.

209
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:45,760
Another experiment could put an end to brown lawn syndrome.

210
00:15:45,761 --> 00:15:48,760
by crossbreeding popular grass varieties

211
00:15:48,761 --> 00:15:50,760
with those that can withstand desert conditions.

212
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:54,760
These grasses are in a drought test.

213
00:15:54,761 --> 00:15:57,760
They look the same now. They have the same soil.

214
00:15:57,761 --> 00:15:59,760
The difference is we are starting to withhold the water.

215
00:15:59,761 --> 00:16:01,760
After a period of time, some of them will die,

216
00:16:01,761 --> 00:16:04,760
some of them will just turn brown and maybe green up later

217
00:16:04,761 --> 00:16:06,760
when they have water.

218
00:16:06,761 --> 00:16:09,760
The key is we're trying to find the ones that are going to last.

219
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:12,760
I think that the beautiful patch of green grass

220
00:16:12,761 --> 00:16:14,760
we've all come to know as a lawn

221
00:16:14,761 --> 00:16:19,260
is an idea that dates back to the Romans and antiquity.

222
00:16:19,261 --> 00:16:23,940
But surprisingly, it's a fairly recent phenomenon.

223
00:16:23,941 --> 00:16:27,200
The word lawn dates to 16th century Europe

224
00:16:27,201 --> 00:16:31,000
and the Old English word lawned, meaning open space.

225
00:16:31,001 --> 00:16:33,880
It simply was an area that livestock grazed

226
00:16:33,881 --> 00:16:38,120
and that grazing kept the grass short.

227
00:16:38,121 --> 00:16:43,920
And that short crop grass is really the genesis of lawns.

228
00:16:43,921 --> 00:16:47,520
Prior to the 16th century, there were few lawns in Europe.

229
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:53,280
Pastures and public green spaces contained a smorgasbord of grass varieties.

230
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:57,280
The grass types were quite different than what we see here.

231
00:16:57,600 --> 00:17:04,720
In fact, some of the grasses, or early lawns rather, were actually things like chamomile or clover and meadow grasses.

232
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:08,700
And these were things that were native to northern Europe at the time.

233
00:17:08,701 --> 00:17:15,260
French landscape architect André La Noche designed one of the first ornamental lawns

234
00:17:15,261 --> 00:17:18,920
in 1661 at the Gardens of Versailles near Paris.

235
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,700
Soon after, British noblemen began planting formal English gardens.

236
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:30,680
But for the English peasant class, the lawn was still an impractical dream.

237
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:35,400
The grass wasn't necessarily greener in the American colonies.

238
00:17:35,401 --> 00:17:42,520
Native grasses like broom straw and wild rye didn't have the nutrients to sustain the grazing

239
00:17:42,521 --> 00:17:43,780
animals brought from Europe.

240
00:17:43,781 --> 00:17:51,900
So very early on in the 1600s, colonists were riding home to Europe asking for clover

241
00:17:51,901 --> 00:17:54,440
and grass seed to be sent.

242
00:17:54,441 --> 00:17:59,140
Many of the colonists' animals starved to death over the winter before the colonists

243
00:17:59,141 --> 00:18:03,920
could get grass seed that would sustain them.

244
00:18:03,921 --> 00:18:08,940
Even with imported European grass, a low-cut English-style lawn didn't terminate on American

245
00:18:08,941 --> 00:18:12,240
soil until after the Civil War.

246
00:18:12,241 --> 00:18:19,100
Then, by the late 1800s, the lawn took root, at least in the city.

247
00:18:19,101 --> 00:18:26,360
Mechanical lawnmowers, fertilizer, and access to city water made growing grass affordable.

248
00:18:26,361 --> 00:18:31,500
The lawn also got a boost from the people who depended on lush grass, the United States

249
00:18:31,501 --> 00:18:33,400
Golf Association.

250
00:18:33,401 --> 00:18:36,800
Thanks to its research, improved strains of Kentucky bluegrass

251
00:18:36,801 --> 00:18:38,540
and other varieties hit the market.

252
00:18:40,700 --> 00:18:43,580
But it took a suburb to spark a yard revolution.

253
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:48,960
In 1947, Levittown became one of the first suburban

254
00:18:48,961 --> 00:18:50,380
tract home developments.

255
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:54,860
Levittown was built in potato fields in Long Island.

256
00:18:54,861 --> 00:18:55,920
There were no trees.

257
00:18:56,780 --> 00:19:00,260
And the easiest way to cover the dirt

258
00:19:00,261 --> 00:19:02,660
and the construction scars was grass.

259
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:08,400
Soon, middle-class America was obsessed with grass lawns.

260
00:19:08,401 --> 00:19:10,400
Everyone wanted one,

261
00:19:10,401 --> 00:19:13,400
and the Scots Company was happy to deliver.

262
00:19:13,401 --> 00:19:18,400
Founded by Civil War veteran O.M. Scott in 1868,

263
00:19:18,401 --> 00:19:21,400
the company learned that the quickest way to a man's heart

264
00:19:21,401 --> 00:19:23,400
was through his golf clubs.

265
00:19:23,401 --> 00:19:26,400
They were very early mass marketers.

266
00:19:26,401 --> 00:19:33,400
They bought or obtained the mailing lists for golf clubs.

267
00:19:33,401 --> 00:19:44,620
and they targeted golfers, and they sent out newsletters to tell people how to have a home lawn that looked like a golf course.

268
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:51,840
Today, golf courses and American lawns are cut from the same roll, with a sod cutter.

269
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:53,960
Sod is instant lawn.

270
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:58,500
For those not patient enough to plant it themselves and wait a few weeks for it to develop,

271
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:00,680
the sod just gives you an automatic lawn.

272
00:20:00,681 --> 00:20:04,660
It's nice and green, and some people prefer that.

273
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:07,440
A sod farm operates like a nursery.

274
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:12,780
It grows grass specific to the local climate over a period of 18 months.

275
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:21,020
A sod cutter skims the turf a quarter of an inch below the surface, creating carpet-like rolls.

276
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:25,120
Rolls provide a mature lawn in a matter of minutes.

277
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:29,220
But some homeowners prefer their grass homegrown.

278
00:20:29,221 --> 00:20:33,560
For that, they need pure, straight from the bag grass seed.

279
00:20:33,561 --> 00:20:38,860
It's a good idea to have multiple varieties involved in a seed mix.

280
00:20:38,861 --> 00:20:44,640
We try and mix and match a strength and a weakness against each grass so that if something

281
00:20:44,641 --> 00:20:48,320
happens in a lawn, like a disease for example, it doesn't affect all the varieties, there's

282
00:20:48,321 --> 00:20:50,120
something there to take over.

283
00:20:50,121 --> 00:20:55,440
But some homeowners are not purists.

284
00:20:55,441 --> 00:20:58,854
a yard takes time, and sod can be very costly.

285
00:20:58,855 --> 00:21:02,640
For them, imitation is the sincerest form of ground

286
00:21:02,641 --> 00:21:03,341
cover.

287
00:21:03,342 --> 00:21:10,080
This new lawn in Phoenix, Arizona is getting a buff and polish, but look closer.

288
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:12,474
The grass is fake.

289
00:21:12,475 --> 00:21:18,320
Synthetic grass, or new grass, is a vast improvement on astroturf,

290
00:21:18,321 --> 00:21:22,800
the fake grass fad that carpeted sports fields and yards in the 1970s and 80s.

291
00:21:22,801 --> 00:21:29,900
It was made of polyethylene, an abrasive material with no shock absorption qualities.

292
00:21:31,420 --> 00:21:39,800
With the new grass products versus real grass, in addition to your watering, mowing, fertilizing, thatching,

293
00:21:40,340 --> 00:21:47,820
new grass, the only maintenance required is an occasional leaf blower, plastic rake or broom just to remove debris.

294
00:21:47,821 --> 00:21:55,980
Today's synthetic grass, version 2.0, is made of the less abrasive polypropylene,

295
00:21:55,981 --> 00:22:00,100
with twisted nylon fibers for shock absorption.

296
00:22:00,101 --> 00:22:05,940
Customers can even choose their own imitation variety, rye, fescue, or bluegrass, and have

297
00:22:05,941 --> 00:22:10,240
it pre-mowed to the height they desire.

298
00:22:10,241 --> 00:22:13,940
Synthetic turf is an alternative to natural grass and a possible answer to shrinking

299
00:22:13,941 --> 00:22:14,940
water resources.

300
00:22:14,941 --> 00:22:25,260
Lurking beneath the turf in millions of American yards is a gadget that dispenses the elixir

301
00:22:25,261 --> 00:22:27,380
crucial to the survival of a lawn.

302
00:22:27,381 --> 00:22:31,760
These days, a lot of people are concerned with drought and how we're using water.

303
00:22:31,761 --> 00:22:32,760
We don't want to waste water.

304
00:22:32,761 --> 00:22:36,100
It's a very precious resource.

305
00:22:36,101 --> 00:22:42,280
In many yards, this resource flows through an underground sprinkler system.

306
00:22:42,281 --> 00:22:48,100
The homeowner programs a computerized controller that executes a watering schedule.

307
00:22:48,101 --> 00:22:51,400
But there are some systems that think for themselves.

308
00:22:51,401 --> 00:22:55,940
Their only master is the weather itself.

309
00:22:55,941 --> 00:23:03,620
For these models, the computer links wirelessly with a nearby weather monitoring station.

310
00:23:03,621 --> 00:23:08,760
The weather station measures temperature, humidity, and other data, and then transmits

311
00:23:08,761 --> 00:23:11,580
hourly updates.

312
00:23:11,581 --> 00:23:17,160
The controller receives the data and then tells the sprinkler system when to water and for how long.

313
00:23:17,860 --> 00:23:25,901
It actually knows how much water the landscape is using and puts back only the amount of water that the plants need without any overwatering, without runoff, and

314
00:23:25,902 --> 00:23:26,602
without waste.

315
00:23:27,180 --> 00:23:28,620
Water savings can be tremendous.

316
00:23:29,280 --> 00:23:32,980
We found you can save as much as 30, even 40 percent on your water bill.

317
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:39,280
With savings like that, you can bet smart irrigation will eventually become a standard in the American yard.

318
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:48,040
We've come a long way from the garden hose. In the early 1870s, the B.F. Goodrich

319
00:23:48,041 --> 00:23:53,460
Company began manufacturing the rubber hose. It made effective watering of the

320
00:23:53,461 --> 00:23:59,420
American lawn possible. In the early 1900s, along came the first true

321
00:23:59,421 --> 00:24:05,820
sprinkler, the Plouillette. The water drove a turbine. The turbine distributed the

322
00:24:05,821 --> 00:24:09,120
water stream while turning on an axis.

323
00:24:09,121 --> 00:24:12,320
But better designs lay ahead.

324
00:24:12,321 --> 00:24:16,800
In 1935, businessman Clem LaFetra began marketing a

325
00:24:16,801 --> 00:24:18,800
sprinkler that was first developed to irrigate

326
00:24:18,801 --> 00:24:22,240
California orange groves.

327
00:24:22,241 --> 00:24:26,880
It was called the Horizontal Action Impact Sprinkler.

328
00:24:26,881 --> 00:24:30,220
It marked the beginning of the Rain Bird Company.

329
00:24:30,221 --> 00:24:31,980
The one I'm holding is a Model 60.

330
00:24:31,981 --> 00:24:35,320
It's the first production model that Rain Bird actually

331
00:24:35,321 --> 00:24:36,420
made and sold.

332
00:24:36,421 --> 00:24:41,000
It will go 360 degrees, in other words, a full circle,

333
00:24:41,001 --> 00:24:44,260
as it's spraying the water.

334
00:24:44,261 --> 00:24:46,700
The impact sprinkler has a spring-loaded spoon

335
00:24:46,701 --> 00:24:49,700
arm that repeatedly interrupts the water stream,

336
00:24:49,701 --> 00:24:53,180
creating an evenly spread curtain of water.

337
00:24:53,181 --> 00:24:55,840
As the arm returns, it strikes the sprinkler head,

338
00:24:55,841 --> 00:24:56,900
causing it to rotate.

339
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:02,940
Today's sprinklers go to boot camp at the Rain Bird

340
00:25:02,941 --> 00:25:07,320
testing facility in Tucson, Arizona.

341
00:25:07,321 --> 00:25:09,840
On the demo lawn, both rotary sprinklers

342
00:25:09,841 --> 00:25:13,320
and circular pop-up sprinklers that have survived various water

343
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:15,880
torture tests are taking a final exam.

344
00:25:19,280 --> 00:25:24,020
That includes the 115E, one of the world's most powerful gear

345
00:25:24,021 --> 00:25:26,400
driven rotor sprinklers.

346
00:25:26,401 --> 00:25:30,500
It casts a jet of water as far as 115 feet,

347
00:25:30,501 --> 00:25:34,100
providing 360 degrees of coverage.

348
00:25:34,101 --> 00:25:36,760
For golf courses or backyard megaloins,

349
00:25:36,761 --> 00:25:39,760
just one of these monsters can water an area equivalent

350
00:25:39,761 --> 00:25:41,300
to the size of a football field.

351
00:25:46,500 --> 00:25:50,100
In the test center, Rain Bird's team of hydro engineers

352
00:25:50,101 --> 00:25:55,620
puts the sprinklers' crucial head assemblies through pure hell.

353
00:25:55,621 --> 00:25:57,900
We literally just run them and run them and run them

354
00:25:57,901 --> 00:25:59,020
until they break.

355
00:25:59,021 --> 00:26:03,780
find out why they break, we fix that and continue to run them and make a better product.

356
00:26:03,781 --> 00:26:08,900
Sprinklers can also take a beating in your own backyard.

357
00:26:08,901 --> 00:26:13,620
An accidental water pressure surge, called water hammer, can occur in a long section

358
00:26:13,621 --> 00:26:15,620
of pipe.

359
00:26:15,621 --> 00:26:20,040
The result could be explosive.

360
00:26:20,041 --> 00:26:22,400
The burst test simulates the potential yard disaster.

361
00:26:22,401 --> 00:26:27,520
So we've set up kind of an extreme test out here that can get hundreds of pounds of pressure,

362
00:26:27,521 --> 00:26:32,480
400 plus pounds of pressure, and we make sure that our sprinkler heads can withstand that

363
00:26:32,481 --> 00:26:35,480
kind of pressure.

364
00:26:35,481 --> 00:26:40,280
Rainbird sprinklers perform under pressure, but can they deliver water to every blade

365
00:26:40,281 --> 00:26:42,040
of grass?

366
00:26:42,041 --> 00:26:44,140
This is Rainbird's distribution test.

367
00:26:44,141 --> 00:26:50,460
We have over 1,000 catch cans here, and we're measuring the amount of water in each one.

368
00:26:50,461 --> 00:26:55,400
They're equally spaced apart, and we're trying to get a very smooth, even distribution pattern.

369
00:26:55,401 --> 00:27:00,580
measure the amount of water and we can adjust the nozzle technology so that we get a perfect,

370
00:27:00,581 --> 00:27:05,300
even distribution.

371
00:27:05,301 --> 00:27:08,500
Distribution is never an issue with drip irrigation.

372
00:27:08,501 --> 00:27:14,460
This water-stingy method is designed for trees, bushes, gardens, flowerbeds, or any other part

373
00:27:14,461 --> 00:27:17,080
of the yard, except the grass.

374
00:27:17,081 --> 00:27:22,840
He uses what we call spot watering emitters or small area emitters.

375
00:27:22,841 --> 00:27:25,540
It literally drips the water on drop by drop.

376
00:27:25,541 --> 00:27:27,680
And you run it for much longer periods of time

377
00:27:27,681 --> 00:27:31,420
and just at the base of each individual plant.

378
00:27:31,421 --> 00:27:33,600
Irrigation and its evolving technologies

379
00:27:33,601 --> 00:27:38,240
are the mandatory accessories for a green lawn.

380
00:27:38,241 --> 00:27:40,120
But one trip to the lawn and garden aisle

381
00:27:40,121 --> 00:27:43,880
reveals a garage-clogging collection of yard gadgets

382
00:27:43,881 --> 00:27:47,240
you may not even know that you need.

383
00:27:47,241 --> 00:27:49,720
The tractor supply company showcases the latest

384
00:27:49,721 --> 00:27:53,200
technology for the do-it-yourself backyard pro.

385
00:27:53,201 --> 00:27:58,240
We've got tillers, lawn tractors, chainsaws, trimmers, blowers,

386
00:27:58,241 --> 00:27:59,920
every gadget you can possibly imagine

387
00:27:59,921 --> 00:28:04,180
to use in your yard and to keep it green all summer long.

388
00:28:04,181 --> 00:28:06,340
One versatile gadget is the yard's version

389
00:28:06,341 --> 00:28:09,160
of a Swiss Army knife.

390
00:28:09,161 --> 00:28:10,840
This is a basic line of weed trimmers

391
00:28:10,841 --> 00:28:11,880
everybody's used to seeing.

392
00:28:11,881 --> 00:28:15,120
But the newest thing in the yard is the four-cycle trimmer.

393
00:28:15,121 --> 00:28:16,660
It has a highly efficient motor that

394
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:18,800
keeps the environment clean.

395
00:28:18,801 --> 00:28:25,960
It also has a fast attach system where you can put on the regular weed trimmer end, an

396
00:28:25,961 --> 00:28:28,200
edger.

397
00:28:28,201 --> 00:28:35,800
You can also use a tree pruner, a hedge trimmer, and a blower attachment, all with one unit

398
00:28:35,801 --> 00:28:41,480
and use one motor in your garage as opposed to having different pieces of equipment.

399
00:28:41,481 --> 00:28:46,840
Detested by many, the ear-splitting leaf blower is being converted to a kinder, gentler

400
00:28:46,841 --> 00:28:47,840
tool.

401
00:28:47,841 --> 00:28:50,480
What we have here is the latest in the backpack blowers.

402
00:28:50,481 --> 00:28:54,200
This has 140-mile-an-hour wind speed, a real low vibration

403
00:28:54,201 --> 00:28:56,020
motor, and a smart start spring.

404
00:28:56,021 --> 00:28:57,680
It'll help it easier to start.

405
00:28:57,681 --> 00:28:59,900
A lot less fatigue on you when you don't have all the vibration,

406
00:28:59,901 --> 00:29:02,320
but it does hang off your back and easy controls,

407
00:29:02,321 --> 00:29:05,720
and you can work with it for hours.

408
00:29:05,721 --> 00:29:07,840
The 21st Century Home Store carries

409
00:29:07,841 --> 00:29:10,340
an endless catalog of power gadgets.

410
00:29:10,341 --> 00:29:13,180
To help the do-it-yourselfer keep order in the yard.

411
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:21,240
High above the backyard, a crew is preparing

412
00:29:21,340 --> 00:29:22,580
to bring down a monster.

413
00:29:25,340 --> 00:29:28,780
Trees are the pillars that can give a yard scale.

414
00:29:28,880 --> 00:29:30,980
But sometimes, they must come down.

415
00:29:34,280 --> 00:29:37,800
Bringing a tree down is only half the battle.

416
00:29:37,900 --> 00:29:39,800
The other half is cutting it down to size.

417
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:46,600
The wood chipper is the Godzilla of yard gadgets.

418
00:29:46,601 --> 00:29:50,880
It's designed with rotating drums or discs and sharp knives

419
00:29:50,881 --> 00:29:55,200
that can devour a tree and reduce it to sawdust in a matter of minutes.

420
00:29:56,080 --> 00:29:59,020
Bringing down a tree is a relatively quick job,

421
00:29:59,780 --> 00:30:02,340
but planting a tree and waiting for a shady canopy

422
00:30:02,341 --> 00:30:04,620
could take 15 or 20 years.

423
00:30:07,220 --> 00:30:09,260
For those who can't wait that long,

424
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:12,960
an outfit like the Davey Company can supersize your order.

425
00:30:14,420 --> 00:30:16,520
They're a select fraternity of tree movers.

426
00:30:16,600 --> 00:30:20,500
The Davy crew hasn't met a tree they can't handle.

427
00:30:20,501 --> 00:30:23,980
When the trunk is 24 inches in diameter or larger,

428
00:30:23,981 --> 00:30:25,840
they use a gantry system.

429
00:30:25,841 --> 00:30:30,140
They trench beneath the tree and insert pipes

430
00:30:30,141 --> 00:30:31,680
to act as a lifting platform.

431
00:30:31,681 --> 00:30:35,420
It's a hydraulic process of actually lifting or jacking

432
00:30:35,421 --> 00:30:39,660
the tree up, very similar to what you see in the building movers

433
00:30:39,661 --> 00:30:43,500
or the structural movers around the country that move buildings.

434
00:30:43,501 --> 00:30:46,500
Once we've got the tree lifted vertically,

435
00:30:46,501 --> 00:30:48,500
we have tracks where we can roll it,

436
00:30:48,501 --> 00:30:53,500
or we bring in our trailer that we assemble alongside the tree

437
00:30:53,501 --> 00:30:55,500
and then roll it beneath the tree.

438
00:30:55,501 --> 00:30:59,500
If the tree is less than 12 inches in diameter,

439
00:30:59,501 --> 00:31:02,500
a customized trailer-mounted spade is all they need.

440
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:07,500
The hydraulic spade uses a high-pressure pump

441
00:31:07,501 --> 00:31:09,500
to drive its blades deep beneath the roots.

442
00:31:09,501 --> 00:31:14,380
One scoop, and this tree is ready to roll.

443
00:31:14,381 --> 00:31:20,140
The Davie Company put down roots in the US in 1880.

444
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:25,580
By the late 1920s, the company had perfected a moving method.

445
00:31:25,581 --> 00:31:30,060
They used what was called an Irish whip, or some people call it an English spar.

446
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:35,260
And it was a device where you actually mounted wheels to the tree itself,

447
00:31:35,261 --> 00:31:39,700
and tilted it down and towed it to the receiving site

448
00:31:39,701 --> 00:31:41,940
and then stood it back up and planted it.

449
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:46,900
In this muddy field on a tree farm west of Houston, Texas,

450
00:31:47,560 --> 00:31:50,480
the Davy crew is preparing to move a 40-foot-tall,

451
00:31:50,920 --> 00:31:52,860
50,000-pound red oak tree.

452
00:31:55,020 --> 00:31:57,940
This is one of six trees destined for a single backyard.

453
00:31:59,380 --> 00:32:02,040
Once the crew has excavated the soil around the trunk,

454
00:32:02,660 --> 00:32:04,540
trimming the roots is the biggest concern.

455
00:32:05,260 --> 00:32:08,600
Our initial process is to come in and root prune the tree.

456
00:32:08,700 --> 00:32:10,900
We determined that for this tree to have

457
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:13,140
a successful transplant, we sized the root ball

458
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:15,900
at 12-foot diameter.

459
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,400
The root ball is a neat package encased

460
00:32:18,500 --> 00:32:21,980
in burlap and steel wire.

461
00:32:22,080 --> 00:32:24,920
It's the tree's life support system.

462
00:32:25,020 --> 00:32:27,320
The crew trims the roots to a safe length based

463
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:32,060
on the size of the tree and then drives in steel pipes.

464
00:32:32,061 --> 00:32:36,200
Once we have a significant number of pipes beneath the ball,

465
00:32:36,201 --> 00:32:40,280
we'll mount a beam with U-bolts to hold the pipe in a rigid platform.

466
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:45,540
So essentially we have a grid of pipe, which is our steel lifting device.

467
00:32:47,740 --> 00:32:50,800
Rainstorms have turned the earth at this tree farm into thick mud.

468
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:56,700
The moisture has increased the overall weight by some 5%.

469
00:32:56,701 --> 00:33:03,700
Getting the heavy tree out of the muck is no simple task.

470
00:33:03,701 --> 00:33:10,700
The tightly wound root ball and its support platform will prevent damage during transit.

471
00:33:10,701 --> 00:33:17,700
A tree hurtling down the freeway is officially a wide load and must travel with a police escort.

472
00:33:17,701 --> 00:33:22,700
Upon its arrival in Houston's elegant River Oaks neighborhood,

473
00:33:22,701 --> 00:33:24,540
River Oaks neighborhood.

474
00:33:24,541 --> 00:33:26,300
The team begins the arduous process

475
00:33:26,301 --> 00:33:28,540
of maneuvering the trailer into the backyard.

476
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:33,980
Now it's time for some heavy lifting.

477
00:33:41,020 --> 00:33:43,820
This tree move is called a round ball crane process.

478
00:33:45,660 --> 00:33:50,220
It will use a 120-ton crane to try and lift the 50,000-pound

479
00:33:50,221 --> 00:33:51,260
tree to its new home.

480
00:33:52,700 --> 00:33:55,300
The crane must work in a tight space.

481
00:33:59,180 --> 00:34:04,480
Existing tree limbs and power lines complicate the lift.

482
00:34:04,481 --> 00:34:08,040
We've left the tarp on on purpose so that as we brush by

483
00:34:08,041 --> 00:34:11,020
this existing tree, we don't snag a limb.

484
00:34:11,021 --> 00:34:14,660
Now we're going to pick it up and lift it up and over the house

485
00:34:14,661 --> 00:34:16,660
and set it down in the receiving spot.

486
00:34:19,320 --> 00:34:22,000
The connections are checked and rechecked.

487
00:34:22,001 --> 00:34:24,340
The last thing anyone wants is a 40-foot oak tree

488
00:34:24,341 --> 00:34:25,440
falling into the house.

489
00:34:26,880 --> 00:34:30,040
Once the tree is airborne, all eyes are on the root ball.

490
00:34:31,080 --> 00:34:34,000
As you notice, the root ball is the integrity of the tree.

491
00:34:34,440 --> 00:34:36,120
We treat that like a cake,

492
00:34:36,360 --> 00:34:39,680
and therefore we don't want to crack the icing on the cake.

493
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:42,520
That's why we do all of our lifting from the structural bottom.

494
00:34:43,740 --> 00:34:46,500
With the tree dangling a few feet above its new home,

495
00:34:47,120 --> 00:34:50,720
it's spun to find the right spot that will cast shade near the house.

496
00:34:50,721 --> 00:34:54,680
Well, these limbs are longer. Let's let them go out that way.

497
00:34:54,681 --> 00:34:58,880
That way, to that side. Stop, stop there.

498
00:34:58,881 --> 00:35:03,920
The move is complete, but the work is far from over.

499
00:35:03,921 --> 00:35:09,680
All of the trees that are transplanted essentially are going into a critical care

500
00:35:09,681 --> 00:35:15,080
method at that point in time because we are cutting off a good percentage of the root system.

501
00:35:15,081 --> 00:35:19,640
So we're trying to regenerate new roots once the tree is installed.

502
00:35:19,641 --> 00:35:23,440
The key to root growth is efficient watering

503
00:35:23,441 --> 00:35:25,240
and loose aerated soil.

504
00:35:25,241 --> 00:35:30,080
The crew buries perforated drainage pipe on a slope

505
00:35:30,081 --> 00:35:33,020
to ensure that gravity will carry water away from the tree

506
00:35:33,021 --> 00:35:34,780
and avoid overwatering.

507
00:35:34,781 --> 00:35:40,360
A backfill of loose soil compost will help with the drainage.

508
00:35:40,361 --> 00:35:44,400
Essentially, it's a two to three year process

509
00:35:44,401 --> 00:35:47,200
to regenerate enough of a root system for the tree

510
00:35:47,201 --> 00:35:50,740
to survive on its own without the critical care treatments.

511
00:35:53,940 --> 00:35:56,840
The Davy Company has moving trees down to a science.

512
00:36:02,240 --> 00:36:05,060
The American backyard has long been the home turf

513
00:36:05,061 --> 00:36:06,360
of man's best friend.

514
00:36:09,060 --> 00:36:13,000
But today, a new creature is challenging the dog's domain.

515
00:36:13,001 --> 00:36:16,300
And you don't even have to clean up after him.

516
00:36:16,301 --> 00:36:20,380
Koi fish originated in China, and in the 19th century,

517
00:36:20,381 --> 00:36:22,800
the Japanese bred them for enhanced color.

518
00:36:24,040 --> 00:36:27,120
They are now tenants of a new vision of the American yard,

519
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:28,620
the waterscape.

520
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:33,800
This living, breathing pond is a suburban wilderness adventure

521
00:36:33,801 --> 00:36:36,240
designed to bring out the child in all of us.

522
00:36:38,220 --> 00:36:41,000
Pacific Outdoor Living in La Crescenta, California

523
00:36:41,001 --> 00:36:44,380
has been taking the yard aquatic for more than 20 years.

524
00:36:46,300 --> 00:36:50,300
The ecosystem we create with our pond is a community of plants,

525
00:36:50,301 --> 00:36:55,300
animals, and microorganisms that we introduce into the area,

526
00:36:55,301 --> 00:36:58,300
and they all depend on each other to survive.

527
00:37:01,300 --> 00:37:05,300
Before the tranquility comes the heavy lifting.

528
00:37:05,301 --> 00:37:09,300
Take this project at a new home in Bel Air, California.

529
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:12,300
So basically we have about 55 feet of waterfall.

530
00:37:12,301 --> 00:37:14,300
It starts up here with a spring, and it's going to come here.

531
00:37:14,301 --> 00:37:17,140
And it's gonna come here, we're gonna have a nice cascading waterfall.

532
00:37:17,141 --> 00:37:22,640
We're gonna have 10,000 gallons flowing through here, so it's gonna be a really nice, loud,

533
00:37:22,641 --> 00:37:23,640
noisy type waterfall.

534
00:37:23,641 --> 00:37:27,240
It's gonna be very, very beautiful.

535
00:37:27,241 --> 00:37:33,260
The waterfall will feed into a two-foot deep koi paradise.

536
00:37:33,261 --> 00:37:37,380
First comes a felt underlayment to prevent weeds from invading.

537
00:37:37,381 --> 00:37:40,780
Then a rubber liner will form the bottom of the pond.

538
00:37:40,781 --> 00:37:44,020
Once this is set in place, we set the rocks all the way around.

539
00:37:44,021 --> 00:37:49,460
from the bottom we rock up and get the different levels rocked in and then we pour in gravel

540
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:58,020
just as the dog gets a doghouse there's another pond feature that every koi literally can't live

541
00:37:58,021 --> 00:38:06,900
without so this is basically a koi garage in this area we have different predators there's birds and

542
00:38:06,901 --> 00:38:12,660
coyotes and raccoons and so forth so this gives a place for the the koi to hide and really the water

543
00:38:12,661 --> 00:38:18,260
only needs to be about two feet deep for the koi to actually be protected.

544
00:38:18,261 --> 00:38:21,960
The koi aren't expected to do their own housework.

545
00:38:21,961 --> 00:38:24,620
This pond is self-cleaning.

546
00:38:24,621 --> 00:38:29,300
So this is the skimmer, which is basically a mechanical filter.

547
00:38:29,301 --> 00:38:34,900
So we have the water level is about one inch below this line here.

548
00:38:34,901 --> 00:38:37,680
It allows the leaves to be drawn into the basket.

549
00:38:37,681 --> 00:38:41,260
And the leaves are picked up and then you can take out the basket, pull the leaves out

550
00:38:41,261 --> 00:38:43,500
and rinse it out, dump it.

551
00:38:43,501 --> 00:38:47,140
And then we have a filter mat that sits on top of there.

552
00:38:50,540 --> 00:38:53,240
At the top of the falls, a second waterscape filter

553
00:38:53,241 --> 00:38:56,680
relies on an unlikely collaborator.

554
00:38:56,681 --> 00:38:59,220
Beneficial bacteria is a very important part

555
00:38:59,221 --> 00:39:01,140
of the ecosystem.

556
00:39:01,141 --> 00:39:03,840
It's something that we introduced to the pond

557
00:39:03,841 --> 00:39:06,960
to help with controlling the algae.

558
00:39:06,961 --> 00:39:08,860
It's not going to stop the algae growth,

559
00:39:08,861 --> 00:39:11,800
but it helps starve the algae of its food source.

560
00:39:14,200 --> 00:39:16,700
The bacteria are introduced at the top of the waterfall

561
00:39:16,701 --> 00:39:19,460
in a biofall filter.

562
00:39:19,461 --> 00:39:22,360
The filter contains lava rocks that encourage bacteria

563
00:39:22,361 --> 00:39:24,340
to colonize.

564
00:39:24,341 --> 00:39:26,980
The bacteria consumes enzymes and other nutrients

565
00:39:26,981 --> 00:39:31,460
and minimize the overgrowth of algae in the ecosystem.

566
00:39:31,461 --> 00:39:34,500
The whole stream actually works as a biological filter

567
00:39:34,501 --> 00:39:35,980
as well, too, because you're going

568
00:39:35,981 --> 00:39:37,980
to get bacteria that will colonize

569
00:39:37,981 --> 00:39:41,720
throughout all the gravel and all the surface space on the stream.

570
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:46,980
The koi do their part by feeding along the pond's bottom,

571
00:39:47,400 --> 00:39:49,480
dislodging debris that will end up in the skimmer.

572
00:39:52,500 --> 00:39:54,300
The fish are famously tough

573
00:39:54,301 --> 00:39:56,600
and can easily survive freezing temperatures

574
00:39:56,601 --> 00:39:58,280
by hibernating beneath the ice.

575
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:02,520
This waterscape is complete.

576
00:40:03,500 --> 00:40:04,960
Once the landscaping is finished,

577
00:40:05,360 --> 00:40:07,200
this yard will certainly draw a crowd.

578
00:40:07,980 --> 00:40:16,040
A lot of these areas that we put these water features in is a location where maybe they

579
00:40:16,041 --> 00:40:17,440
weren't using very much.

580
00:40:17,441 --> 00:40:20,280
It was just another patch of grass in their lawn.

581
00:40:20,281 --> 00:40:28,540
But now we actually put a living, breathing organism in that area.

582
00:40:28,541 --> 00:40:32,780
Self-sufficient waterscapes are making some rethink our vision of the traditional yard

583
00:40:32,781 --> 00:40:36,800
as a carpet of green turf.

584
00:40:36,801 --> 00:40:43,440
turf requires resources like fertilizer, pesticides, herbicides, and in most cases,

585
00:40:44,000 --> 00:40:48,480
a great deal of water. What is the future of the American yard?

586
00:40:48,481 --> 00:40:55,440
I think that's the struggle as municipalities look to balance the needs of, you know, the homeowner

587
00:40:55,441 --> 00:41:01,040
and the desire to have a nice lush lawn, the desire to use scarce resources appropriately

588
00:41:01,041 --> 00:41:02,500
and manage them well.

589
00:41:02,501 --> 00:41:06,580
We have created a savanna from coast to coast.

590
00:41:06,581 --> 00:41:09,620
This whole idea of the lawn around the house

591
00:41:09,621 --> 00:41:12,980
has become a national aesthetic.

592
00:41:12,981 --> 00:41:16,520
It's only maybe 150 years old, but this

593
00:41:16,521 --> 00:41:21,360
is what people have been taught to believe.

594
00:41:21,361 --> 00:41:23,000
No matter what you cover it with,

595
00:41:23,001 --> 00:41:28,200
real grass, fake grass, trees, or water wonderlands,

596
00:41:28,201 --> 00:41:31,700
The yard is a refuge and buffer zone.

597
00:41:31,701 --> 00:41:35,740
It's our chance to create a particular vision of nature

598
00:41:35,741 --> 00:41:37,440
right in our own backyard.

